WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.063
A sparse attention layer reads only a few thousand keys per query.

00:00:04.063 --> 00:00:07.032
So why does it still crawl at ten million tokens?

00:00:09.957 --> 00:00:10.838
Hi, I'm Ladle!

00:00:10.838 --> 00:00:14.364
The answer is a hidden bill, and Latent Sparse Attention, or LSA,

00:00:14.364 --> 00:00:15.834
pays far less of it.

00:00:15.834 --> 00:00:17.009
Let's open it up.

00:00:18.675 --> 00:00:19.308
Chapter one.

00:00:19.308 --> 00:00:20.258
The hidden bill.

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:28.490
A new query could look at every earlier key.

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:31.179
Sparse attention reads only a few thousand of them.

00:00:31.599 --> 00:00:34.319
Something has to choose those keys: the indexer.

00:00:34.319 --> 00:00:39.078
In production designs it still scores every earlier key, or every block of four.

00:00:39.498 --> 00:00:41.734
Then attention runs on the chosen few.

00:00:41.734 --> 00:00:43.012
The attention got cheap.

00:00:43.012 --> 00:00:44.290
The choosing did not.

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:47.955
Scoring every key grows with the context, for every query.

00:00:47.955 --> 00:00:50.550
Over a whole prompt, that is quadratic again.

00:00:52.983 --> 00:00:57.718
Here is the bill on GLM-5.3-Flash, measured by the paper on an H200.

00:00:57.718 --> 00:01:02.452
At half a million tokens, the indexer is two thirds of the layer.

00:01:02.872 --> 00:01:07.389
Counting nothing but multiply-adds from the model configs lands in the same place.

00:01:07.809 --> 00:01:11.422
At ten million tokens, ninety-eight percent of prefill is spent choosing

00:01:11.422 --> 00:01:12.736
keys, not reading them.

00:01:12.736 --> 00:01:16.349
That is the shape of the algorithm, not a slow kernel.

00:01:17.883 --> 00:01:19.077
The paper's own figure.

00:01:19.077 --> 00:01:21.167
Full attention in red, sparse in blue.

00:01:21.167 --> 00:01:22.660
Now look at the inset.

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:24.988
The light blue is the indexer.

00:01:24.988 --> 00:01:28.486
A free indexer would make this layer fifty-eight times faster again.

00:01:30.508 --> 00:01:31.246
Chapter two.

00:01:31.246 --> 00:01:32.721
Buckets instead of keys.

00:01:36.642 --> 00:01:37.585
Here's the idea.

00:01:37.585 --> 00:01:40.729
Cluster the keys by meaning into buckets of about eight.

00:01:40.729 --> 00:01:44.501
Then a query scores one centroid per bucket, instead of every key.

00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:50.812
Keys arrive in blocks of five hundred and twelve, each projected to

00:01:50.812 --> 00:01:53.299
sixty-four dimensions and normalised onto a sphere.

00:01:53.719 --> 00:01:57.479
Every four neighbouring keys are summed into one pool key: a hundred

00:01:57.479 --> 00:01:58.733
and twenty-eight per block.

00:01:59.153 --> 00:02:03.217
Ward's method merges them bottom up, always joining the pair that adds

00:02:03.217 --> 00:02:05.588
the least scatter, down to sixty-four buckets.

00:02:06.008 --> 00:02:08.994
About eight keys a bucket, never more than sixteen.

00:02:08.994 --> 00:02:12.976
Each keeps one centroid and its key positions, and never changes again.

00:02:14.967 --> 00:02:16.180
Now a query arrives.

00:02:16.180 --> 00:02:19.821
Its heads are summed into one index query, so one list of

00:02:19.821 --> 00:02:21.034
keys serves every head.

00:02:21.454 --> 00:02:25.815
It scores the centroid of every earlier bucket: one dot product per eight keys.

00:02:26.235 --> 00:02:28.947
It keeps the best one thousand and twenty-four buckets.

00:02:29.367 --> 00:02:34.053
It reads them best first, appending keys until it holds four thousand and ninety-six.

00:02:34.473 --> 00:02:38.340
Add four sink keys and a five hundred and twelve key window:

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:42.530
attention reads four thousand six hundred and twelve keys, with an unchanged kernel.

00:02:44.717 --> 00:02:46.603
The paper draws the same path.

00:02:46.603 --> 00:02:48.488
Clustering happens once per block, here.

00:02:48.908 --> 00:02:52.806
The top buckets expand into sink, long-range and window indices.

00:02:53.226 --> 00:02:56.594
And on the right, a tree, for when scoring every bucket is too much.

00:02:58.175 --> 00:02:59.949
Flat LSA scores every bucket.

00:02:59.949 --> 00:03:04.207
At ten million tokens that is over a million centroids per query.

00:03:04.627 --> 00:03:08.752
The tree groups buckets into node buckets of four children, level on level.

00:03:08.752 --> 00:03:11.925
At ten million tokens the beam starts at level three.

00:03:12.345 --> 00:03:15.912
It keeps the best two thousand and forty-eight nodes, steps down to

00:03:15.912 --> 00:03:17.993
their children, and keeps that many again.

00:03:18.413 --> 00:03:22.784
About forty-five thousand vectors scored instead of one point three million.

00:03:22.784 --> 00:03:23.976
Twenty-nine times fewer.

00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:26.349
Chapter three.

00:03:26.349 --> 00:03:27.446
Does it hold up?

00:03:33.233 --> 00:03:37.608
One layer at ten million tokens is seventy-two times faster than full attention.

00:03:37.608 --> 00:03:41.647
But a layer is not a model: end to end on Qwen,

00:03:41.647 --> 00:03:45.013
time to first token improves nine point two one times.

00:03:45.433 --> 00:03:49.126
And the authors' own block-sparse baseline is faster end to end

00:03:49.126 --> 00:03:50.469
below a million tokens.

00:03:50.889 --> 00:03:53.093
LSA pulls ahead only at ten million.

00:03:53.093 --> 00:03:56.557
It is a long-context method, and its own table says so.

00:03:58.025 --> 00:04:01.979
The article's own experiments, on synthetic keys, found the soft spots.

00:04:02.399 --> 00:04:04.997
Pooling assumes neighbours mean the same thing.

00:04:04.997 --> 00:04:09.451
On scattered keys at sixty-four k, recall fell to fifty-two point three

00:04:09.451 --> 00:04:12.049
percent, against ninety-nine point four without it.

00:04:12.469 --> 00:04:17.151
Blocks of four, scoring twice as many vectors, beat shipped LSA almost everywhere.

00:04:17.571 --> 00:04:20.956
And each level the tree's beam descends costs a little recall, with

00:04:20.956 --> 00:04:22.931
a beam already at the engine's limit.

00:04:24.808 --> 00:04:27.975
Past a million tokens, choosing keys is the bill.

00:04:27.975 --> 00:04:32.900
LSA pays it once, by clustering, so every query searches buckets instead of keys.

00:04:34.317 --> 00:04:34.968
To recap.

00:04:34.968 --> 00:04:37.899
At ten million tokens, the indexer is the bill.

00:04:37.899 --> 00:04:41.482
Buckets of about eight let a query score one centroid each.

00:04:41.482 --> 00:04:44.087
And it pays off past a million tokens.

00:04:44.507 --> 00:04:46.574
Every source is in the full article.

00:04:46.574 --> 00:04:47.164
I'm Ladle.

00:04:47.164 --> 00:04:47.460
Bye!
